 $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186134283 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$\n- $V=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$\n- $$\nW=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186134518 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf{x}=[{x}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186152579 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186152580 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186152581 "Consider the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$."] [17592186152582 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$,  ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $A.$\nDetermine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186152583 "Given the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$."] [17592186152721 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $A$.\nDetermine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$.\nIf that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186184065 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186184761 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ with coefficients in $\\mathbb{R}$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186184762 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha \\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha (\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0})+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186184763 "Let $C$ be the set of functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186184764 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{x}$ for some $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186240977 "Let $\\mathbf